WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.640
﻿Hi how you doing? I’m going to teach you&nbsp;
how to make sense of Feynman diagrams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.360
know what all the squiggly lines mean,&nbsp;
and work out if they are representing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:16.920
a valid particle interaction or if they&nbsp;
are defying the laws of physics. Ugh.
&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.040
A Feynman diagram is a picture of what happens&nbsp;
when particles interact with each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.120
Particles go in one end, come out&nbsp;
the other, and some stuff happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.320
in the middle. And to make sense of Feynman&nbsp;
diagrams you need to know three things.
&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.800
What all the lines and arrows mean.
What all the particles in the standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.720
model are as well as their special&nbsp;
properties like charge and spin.
&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.600
What the overall rules are. These are called&nbsp;
conservation rules, but you could call them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.000
in-out rules, as in, you can’t get out a&nbsp;
different amount out than what you put in.
&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.600
Here’s a Feynman diagram. The lines represent&nbsp;
different particles which are travelling through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.720
space and time which are represented by these&nbsp;
axes. Note that with one dimension of space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.440
we are losing some spatial information here so&nbsp;
these really are schematics of what a particle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.920
interaction looks like rather than an accurate&nbsp;
depiction of our four dimensional reality.
&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.000
Here’s an example with a little animation to go&nbsp;
with it. Two electrons move towards each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.280
exchange a photon, and move&nbsp;
away from each other again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.840
and that’s what this looks&nbsp;
like as a Feynman diagram.
&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.320
In this video I’m drawing time going upwards,&nbsp;
but some people draw it going sideways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.440
which is quite annoying. So, the first thing&nbsp;
when you look at a Feynman diagram is to check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.640
which way time is going, hopefully they tell&nbsp;
you and if they don’t they are bad people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.880
Drawing time going upwards is the most&nbsp;
common way so that’s what I’m doing here. 
&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.960
Okay that’s the basics, now let’s go onto&nbsp;
part one of the three things we need to know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.520
What are the squiggly lines.
These are the four kinds of lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.720
each corresponding to a kind of particle.&nbsp;
And I can only explain this if I show you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.640
all the particles, so here they are.
These are the fundamental particles of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.360
Universe that everything is made&nbsp;
of (as far as we know so far).
&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.080
But why are they organised like this&nbsp;
instead of being organised like this? Ugh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.160
It’s because some particles share similar&nbsp;
properties so we can group them together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.800
Kind of like my cheese collection.
The straight lines represent the fermions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:32.880
which are these particles. These other particles&nbsp;
are all called bosons. Now the difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.480
fermions and bosons at a basic level is the&nbsp;
fermions make up the stuff in the universe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:44.000
the bosons are how the stuff interacts with each&nbsp;
other and they are called force carriers.
&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.320
In fact, everything we experience is made of&nbsp;
just these three fermions: the up and down quarks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:56.400
which make protons and neutrons, and when you add&nbsp;
electrons to protons and neutrons you get atoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.720
that we’re made of and everything&nbsp;
is made of and you’re made of.
&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.040
The bosons, the force carries,&nbsp;
are all the other lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.840
We use a squiggly line for the photon, which is&nbsp;
the force carrier for the electromagnetic field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.012 --> 00:03:19.852
The spiral lines are for gluons which carry the&nbsp;
strong force that keeps the nucleus of atoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.852 --> 00:03:24.892
together and interacts only with these specific&nbsp;
fermions which are called the quarks.
&nbsp;

00:03:25.692 --> 00:03:32.412
Now onto the weak force. The mediators of the weak&nbsp;
force are the W and Z bosons and are represented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:37.372
with a dashed line. All particles interact&nbsp;
with the weak force apart from the gluons.
&nbsp;

00:03:37.372 --> 00:03:41.932
If this is all a bit much to take in don’t worry&nbsp;
I’ve made a companion poster which you can study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.932 --> 00:03:47.612
in your own time the link in below.
Finally there is the Higgs boson which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.612 --> 00:03:55.532
also use a dashed line for. And those are all the&nbsp;
lines. What about gravity the fourth fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.532 --> 00:03:56.772
force?.
Haha,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.692 --> 00:04:00.732
we don’t have gravity here.&nbsp;
This is particle physics!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.612 --> 00:04:08.492
You’re supposed to ask about the arrows.
But what about the arrows? Thanks for asking!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.492 --> 00:04:13.212
These only appear on the solid lines of&nbsp;
the fermions, and they point forwards in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.212 --> 00:04:18.092
time for matter particles and backwards&nbsp;
in time for anti-matter particles.
&nbsp;

00:04:18.092 --> 00:04:22.972
Because weirdly, in particle physics, an&nbsp;
antimatter particle is exactly the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.972 --> 00:04:29.212
as a matter particle that’s travelling backwards&nbsp;
in time. But where on my little picture are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.212 --> 00:04:34.012
antimatter particles? Oh yeah, nobody ever&nbsp;
bothers to draw them on the standard model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.732 --> 00:04:38.732
so I’ve done it here, here they all are.
There are antimatter copies of all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.732 --> 00:04:43.532
particles, which have the same mass as the matter&nbsp;
versions, but opposite everything else.
&nbsp;

00:04:45.052 --> 00:04:50.332
And while we’re actually drawing actually all the&nbsp;
particles I may as well draw the different colour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.332 --> 00:04:56.812
charges of the quarks and the eight kinds of&nbsp;
gluons. These colours aren’t real by the way just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.812 --> 00:05:02.412
our labelling system for keeping track of&nbsp;
the rules of the strong force. So this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.412 --> 00:05:09.372
actually all of the fundamental particles.
Goodness, there are a lot of rules aren’t there?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.372 --> 00:05:13.852
I’m sorry it’s all so complicated,&nbsp;
but that’s just life isn’t it?
&nbsp;

00:05:15.132 --> 00:05:20.652
So now we’ve covered part one and part two: what&nbsp;
the squiggly lines mean and what all the particles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.652 --> 00:05:25.852
are. This is enough to be able to read Feynman&nbsp;
diagrams and make sense of what is going on.
&nbsp;

00:05:25.852 --> 00:05:28.412
So let’s apply this knowledge&nbsp;
with some examples.
&nbsp;

00:05:29.532 --> 00:05:34.012
Here’s one we met earlier showing two electrons&nbsp;
interacting with a photon and is an example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.012 --> 00:05:38.972
an interaction via the electromagnetic&nbsp;
force. Here are a couple more but these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.972 --> 00:05:43.532
are interactions involving the weak force and&nbsp;
here’s a couple involving the strong force.
&nbsp;

00:05:44.412 --> 00:05:48.732
You’ll notice that these are all a similar shape,&nbsp;
and I did that on purpose because I want to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.732 --> 00:05:54.252
about vertices. If you chop these in half you&nbsp;
get a vertex, which is basically a meeting point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.252 --> 00:06:00.172
between three particles, and you can think of&nbsp;
these vertices as the building blocks of Feynman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.172 --> 00:06:06.092
diagrams. You can build most feynman diagrams&nbsp;
out of this basis set of vertices, which I’m not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.092 --> 00:06:10.172
going to describe in detail now, but pause the&nbsp;
video now if you want to study them more.
&nbsp;

00:06:10.892 --> 00:06:14.732
What is interesting about these is that they&nbsp;
mean different things depending on how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.732 --> 00:06:21.532
are oriented in space and time. For example, this&nbsp;
is an electron that has a change of momentum when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.532 --> 00:06:27.372
it emits a photon. But if I rotate it this&nbsp;
electron becomes a positron, remember that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.372 --> 00:06:32.332
rule about matter travelling backwards in time is&nbsp;
just antimatter travelling forwards in time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.332 --> 00:06:37.932
then this shows a positron and an electron coming&nbsp;
together to annihilate into a photon. So different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.932 --> 00:06:43.692
orientations can mean very different things.
So with this basis set of vertices put in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.692 --> 00:06:47.852
different orientations, you’ve got pretty much&nbsp;
all you need to build any feynman diagram.
&nbsp;

00:06:49.132 --> 00:06:54.092
Let’s look at some more complicated examples.&nbsp;
Here are the electrons interacting again, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.092 --> 00:06:58.492
in the middle the photon spontaneously&nbsp;
turns into an electron positron pair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.492 --> 00:07:02.172
before annihilating and turning&nbsp;
into a photon again.
&nbsp;

00:07:02.172 --> 00:07:07.052
See if you can work out what’s going on here? This&nbsp;
is a neutron, which is made of three quarks: up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.052 --> 00:07:14.412
down, down. It decays into a proton via the weak&nbsp;
force. A proton is made of up, down, up quarks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.412 --> 00:07:19.372
The w boson that is emitted then decays into&nbsp;
an electron and an anti-electron neutrino.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.172 --> 00:07:24.972
So this is how neutrons can turn into protons&nbsp;
via the weak force and is known in nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.972 --> 00:07:29.852
physics as a beta decay emission.
This last one I’m going to leave to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.852 --> 00:07:34.972
to disentangle. See if you can work out what is&nbsp;
going on here and specifically can you answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.972 --> 00:07:40.412
which force is mediating this interaction? Let me&nbsp;
know in the comments below what you think..
